  clock: 1  # 15b8
  nonce: 15b8  # 15b8
  data:  # 15b8
    kind: work_item  # 15b8
    title: "Tracker validation: workspace/canonical items must not reference stealth-tier items via parent / isbefore / duplicate_of (cross-tier dangling refs)"  # 15b8
    status: todo_hard  # 15b8
    priority: 1  # 15b8
    parent:  # 15b8
    tags:  # 15b8
    - governance  # 15b8
    - tracker  # 15b8
    isbefore: []  # 15b8
    duplicate_of: []  # 15b8
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 15b8
    pr_ref:  # 15b8
    description: "# Symptom\n\nThe validation in `packages/hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/validation.py:639`\n(`_check_dangling_parents`) and the matching `_check_before_cycles` /\nisbefore-resolution logic operate on the union of `tracker/.ops`,\n`tracker-workspace/.ops`, and `tracker-workspace/stealth/` — fine\nlocally, but `tracker-workspace/stealth/.gitignore` excludes `*.ops`,\nso CI's checkout never sees stealth items. Workspace items that\nreference a stealth parent (or `isbefore`, or `duplicate_of`,\nor `not_duplicate_of`) silently pass `tracker validate` locally and\nthen fail in CI with `dangling parent reference`.\n\nThis was first encountered on PR #3365 (the regression item WI-rubir\nspawned by `bakeoff-deep-reflect aggregate` referencing stealth-tier\nWI-darad as its parent). The aggregator uses\n`tracker add --parent <id>` blindly and does not check the parent's\ntier; the `add` CLI itself accepts the cross-tier reference because\nits `_resolve_ref` resolves stealth IDs successfully via `ts._resolve_id`\n(stealth items are visible to local tracker reads).\n\n# Bug class — all four reference fields, three tier pairs\n\nThe bug is structural, not specific to parent-references. Every\nref-typed field on `CompiledItem` participates:\n\n- `parent`\n- `isbefore` (list)\n- `duplicate_of` (list)\n- `not_duplicate_of` (list)\n\nFor each, three tier-pair combinations are interesting:\n\n| child / referrer tier | target tier | symptom |\n|---|---|---|\n| workspace | stealth | CI fails: dangling reference (target gitignored) |\n| canonical | stealth | same — canonical is committed, can never reference a local-only id |\n| canonical | workspace | CI passes (workspace IS committed) but is a tier-precedence smell — canonical is supposed to be authoritative; workspace items are draft-tier. If the workspace target is later deleted/demoted, the canonical reference dangles. |\n\nThe `workspace → workspace`, `workspace → canonical`, and stealth-only\ncombinations are fine.\n\n# Likely fixes (pick the layer; multiple may compose)\n\n1. **At the CLI / `tracker add | update`.** When `--parent`,\n   `--add-isbefore`, etc. resolves to a stealth-tier item, refuse\n   unless the new/edited item is also in stealth. Same for\n   canonical → workspace (warn at minimum, or refuse). Direct, but\n   doesn't help items already created via `batch` or `_apply_op`\n   programmatic paths.\n\n2. **At `compile_ops` / write time.** Every op that writes a ref-typed\n   field could check the target's tier vs the writing item's tier and\n   reject. Centralised enforcement; survives any caller path.\n\n3. **At the aggregator (`bakeoff-deep-reflect spawn_regression`).**\n   The first-known offender. Should look up the parent's tier and\n   either spawn the regression in the same tier or skip the\n   `--parent` arg and put the reference in the description only.\n   Narrow fix; doesn't catch other future offenders.\n\n4. **In validation, drop the false-clean local-pass.** Have\n   `tracker validate` (and the pre-commit hook) iterate the same\n   tier set CI sees by default — i.e. SKIP the stealth tier unless\n   the user passes `--include-stealth`. Workspace items with stealth\n   refs would then fail locally too, matching CI's behaviour.\n\n(1) + (4) is the minimum: prevent introducing new cross-tier refs at\nwrite time AND surface existing ones at validate time. (3) is the\nimmediate-mitigation while (1)/(4) ship.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- New tests in `test_validation.py` cover all four reference fields\n  across the three tier-pair combinations listed above; the\n  workspace→stealth case fails the new validation; canonical→stealth\n  fails; canonical→workspace warns (or fails under strict).\n- `tracker validate` and the pre-commit hook reproduce the CI failure\n  signal locally — i.e. PR #3365's failure would have been caught\n  before push.\n- `bakeoff-deep-reflect aggregate --apply-verdicts` no longer creates\n  workspace regression items with stealth parents.\n\n# Out of scope\n\n- Changing the stealth tier's gitignore (it's local-by-design per the\n  ADR).\n- Promoting / demoting items between tiers as part of the validation\n  fix (that requires human authority).\n\n# Source\n\nSurfaced by PR #3365 CI failure on commit fc48ca2e87cc:\n\n```\nERROR: WI-rubir-...: dangling parent reference 'WI-darad-...'\n```\n\nRoot cause analysis recorded in this work item's discussion and in\nthe corrective commit on PR #3365."  # 15b8
    fields: {}  # 15b8
- op: discuss  # ee26
  at: '2026-04-25T20:10:37Z'  # ee26
  by: agent  # ee26
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ee26
  clock: 2  # ee26
  nonce: ee26  # ee26
  message: "Correction to fix #4 (re: user feedback). Earlier framing of '--include-stealth opt-in' was wrong: stealth items must always be well-formed, not opt-in. The correct design splits validation into per-item structural checks (run always on every tier) and cross-tier reference-resolution checks (run with two index scopes, switched on the source item's tier):\n\n- Items in canonical or workspace must have refs that resolve in (canonical union workspace) — i.e. CI's view. A workspace item pointing at a stealth-tier id fails locally too, matching CI.\n- Items in stealth must have refs that resolve in (canonical union workspace union stealth) — the full local index. Stealth items can validly point at workspace items (CI sees those independently), at canonical items, or at other stealth items. A stealth ref to a non-existent id is still a real bug, surfaced under the local scope.\n\nPer-item structural validation (op format, clock ordering, nonces, required fields) does not change — it iterates every ops file regardless of tier.\n\nThe asymmetry is purely: which target-set is each tier's REFERENCE-fields allowed to resolve into. This catches the workspace->stealth class (CI failure) AND keeps stealth's own integrity guarantees, without making stealth validation optional."  # ee26
